'''5.''' Drenched or drunk with liquor; tipsy. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|When my lost Lover the tall Ship ascends,<br>With Music gay, and ''wet'' with jovial Friends.
{{right|''Prior'', Celia to Damon.}}}}

'''6.''' In ''U. S. polit. slang'', opposed to prohibition of the manufacture and sale of intoxicating liquors: as, a ''wet'' town. Compare ''dry'', 13.—'''A wet blanket.''' {{smaller|See ''blanket''.}}—'''A wet boat,''' {{smaller|a boat that is crank and ships water readily.}}
{{smaller block|“Why don’t you go forward, sir? . . . she is sure to wet us abaft.” . . . “Thank you, but . . . (with an heroic attempt at sea-slang) I like ''a wet boat''.”
{{right|''C. Reade'', Love me Little, xvii.}}}}
'''A wet day.''' {{smaller|Same as ''a rainy day'' (which see, under ''rainy'').}}
{{smaller block|Ergo, saith the miser, “part with nothing, but keep all against ''a wet day''.”
{{right|''Fuller'', General Worthies, xi. (''Davies.'')}}}}
'''Wet bargain.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Dutch bargain'' (which see, under ''bargain'').}}—'''Wet bob,''' {{smaller|a boy who goes in for boating in preference to cricket, foot-ball, or other land-sports. [Eton College slang.]}}
{{smaller block|Everything is enjoyable at Eton in the summer half. The ''wet-bobs'' on the river, in all their many trials of strength, . . . and the “dry-bobs” in the playing-fields, with all the excitement of their countless matches.
{{right|''C. E. Pascoe'', Every-day Life in Our Public Schools, p. 62.}}}}
'''Wet brain,''' {{smaller|a dropsical condition of the brain and its membranes, sometimes observed in post-mortem examinations of those who have died of delirium tremens.}}—'''Wet-bulb thermometer.''' {{smaller|See ''psychrometer'' (with cut).}}—'''Wet cooper.''' {{smaller|See ''cooper''.}}—'''Wet dock,''' {{smaller|a dock or basin at a seaport furnished with gates for shutting in the tidal water, so as to float vessels berthed in it at a proper level for loading and unloading.}}—'''Wet goods,''' {{smaller|liquors:}}

