'''wet'''<sup>1</sup> (wet), ''n.'' [E. dial. and Sc. also ''weet'' and ''wat''; < ME. ''wet, wete, wæte, wate'', < AS. ''wǣta'', m., ''wǣte'', f. (= Icel. Sw. ''vǣta'' = Dan. ''væde''), wet, moisture, < ''wǣt'', wet: see ''wet''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] '''1.''' That which makes wet, as water and other liquids; moisture; specifically, rain.
{{smaller block|<poem>I se wel how ye swete;
Have heer a cloth and wype awey the ''wete.''</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Canon’s Yeoman’s Tale, l. 176.}}
<poem>Upon whose [a river’s] weeping margent she was set;
Like usury, applying ''wet'' to ''wet.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lover’s Complaint, l. 40.}}
<poem>Aft ha’e I run your errands, lady,
When blawin baith wind and ''weet.''</poem>
{{right|''Lady Maisry'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 83).}}
The gable-end of the cottage was stained with ''wet.''
{{right|''T. Hardy'', Three Strangers.}}}}
'''2.''' The act of wetting; specifically, a wetting of the throat with drink; a drink or dram of liquor; indulgence in drinking. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|No bargain could be completed without a ''wet'', and no friendship or enmity forgotten without recourse to the bottle. {{right|''A. C. Grant'', Bush-Life in Queensland, I. 30.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''U. S. polit. slang'', an opponent of prohibition; one who favors the traffic in liquor.—

