{{smaller block|The system of ''wetnursing'' adopted by the Post Office authorities in the case of the telegraph service has not been one of uniform success. {{right|''Elect. Rev.'' (Eng.), XXVII. 205.}}}}

'''wet-pack''' (wet′pak), ''n.'' A means of reducing the temperature in fever by wrapping the body in cloths wet with cold water, and covering these with a blanket or other dry material.

'''wet-press''' (wet′pres), ''n.'' In ''paper-making'', the second press in which wet hand-made paper is compacted and partially dried. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''wet-salter†''' (wet′sâl″tėr), ''n.'' A salter who prepares or deals in wet provisions. See ''wet provisions'', under ''wet''<sup>1</sup>. Compare ''dry-salter''.
{{smaller block|The Parade . . . smelt as strong about Breakfast Times as a ''Wet Salter’s'' Shop at Midsummer. {{right|''Tom Brown'', Works (ed. 1708), III. 86.}}}}

'''wet-shod''' (wet′shod), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wet-shod'', ''wat-shod'', ''wete-shodde''; < ''wet''<sup>1</sup> + ''shod''<sup>1</sup>.] Wet as regards the shoes; wearing wet shoes.
{{smaller block|<poem>There [in the battle] men were ''wetschoede''
Alle of Brayn & of blode.</poem>
{{right|''Arthur'' (ed. Furnivall), l. 469.}}}}
{{smaller block|Unless to shame his Court Flatterers who would not else be convinc’t, Canute needed not to have gone ''wet-shod'' home. {{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., vi.}}}}
{{smaller block|So he went over at last, not much aboue ''wet-shod''. {{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, ii.}}}}

'''wetter''' (wet′ėr), ''n.'' One who wets, or practises wetting, for some purpose; specifically, in ''printing'', a workman who wets down paper. See phrase under ''wet''<sup>1</sup>, ''v. t.''

